1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:07,850
Four posts, a sill, a rail between
each pair, and a beam across the top.

2
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:11,330
Every joint closes on the first try.

3
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:20,320
So how does every piece
fit the first time,

4
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:23,050
when nobody measured them with a tape?

5
00:00:24,233 --> 00:00:26,743
Most of us lay out with a tape.

6
00:00:27,193 --> 00:00:30,523
Hook the end, read the
number, make a mark.

7
00:00:31,153 --> 00:00:33,703
Then the next piece, and the next.

8
00:00:34,393 --> 00:00:38,093
Every piece gets its own
measurement, and every

9
00:00:38,133 --> 00:00:42,003
measurement is a fresh
chance to be slightly off.

10
00:00:42,567 --> 00:00:45,957
Traditional Japanese
carpentry leans the other way.

11
00:00:46,247 --> 00:00:51,997
Each position is decided once, on a single
reference, and copied onto every piece.

12
00:00:52,607 --> 00:00:54,897
After that, nothing is measured again.

13
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:01,170
This marking out of the
timber is called sumitsuke:

14
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:06,170
drawing every line in ink
before a saw touches the wood.

15
00:01:06,740 --> 00:01:09,490
It is where the fit of a joint is decided.

16
00:01:11,467 --> 00:01:14,817
But copying needs something to copy from.

17
00:01:15,647 --> 00:01:18,877
Real timber is never quite
the size on the drawing.

18
00:01:19,487 --> 00:01:23,557
One post comes out a few
millimeters thinner than the next.

19
00:01:23,947 --> 00:01:27,877
It may not be square,
and it moves as it dries.

20
00:01:28,667 --> 00:01:33,587
Lay out from a face, and that
face lines up perfectly, but

21
00:01:33,627 --> 00:01:37,277
every difference in size
lands on the other side.

22
00:01:37,747 --> 00:01:41,187
A post a few millimeters
thin ends up with its

23
00:01:41,227 --> 00:01:44,017
middle off the line its neighbors share.

24
00:01:44,567 --> 00:01:48,587
On a Japanese plan, the lines
that place the posts and

25
00:01:48,627 --> 00:01:52,187
walls usually run through
the middle of the members.

26
00:01:52,227 --> 00:01:55,617
So the carpenter lays
out from the middle too:

27
00:01:56,227 --> 00:02:01,297
each member gets a center line,
the shinzumi, struck in ink.

28
00:02:01,667 --> 00:02:05,317
A thinner post still has
its center on the line.

29
00:02:05,887 --> 00:02:09,097
The difference splits, half to each side.

30
00:02:13,833 --> 00:02:17,363
Where a Western woodworker
marks a reference face,

31
00:02:17,673 --> 00:02:21,023
the main Japanese layout
reference is this line.

32
00:02:21,553 --> 00:02:25,163
In this series, a center
line always looks like this.

33
00:02:26,967 --> 00:02:29,457
Now the story stick itself.

34
00:02:30,087 --> 00:02:32,297
You may know it from Western shops:

35
00:02:32,787 --> 00:02:36,597
a straight, plain batten, cut
to the length of the post.

36
00:02:37,767 --> 00:02:41,957
On it, you mark every
position the post needs, once.

37
00:02:42,447 --> 00:02:43,637
The bottom end.

38
00:02:43,947 --> 00:02:46,637
The top of the sill, where the post seats.

39
00:02:46,987 --> 00:02:49,157
The bottom and top of the rail.

40
00:02:49,707 --> 00:02:51,437
The underside of the beam.

41
00:02:51,907 --> 00:02:52,907
The top end.

42
00:02:53,547 --> 00:02:57,197
Beside each line, a short
note says what it is for.

43
00:02:57,667 --> 00:03:00,337
More on the marking tools, next time.

44
00:03:04,633 --> 00:03:08,833
Then the stick goes against
the first post, ends

45
00:03:08,873 --> 00:03:12,963
flush, and each line is
carried straight across.

46
00:03:17,100 --> 00:03:18,410
The second post.

47
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:21,030
Same stick, same lines.

48
00:03:23,433 --> 00:03:24,433
The third.

49
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:29,370
And the fourth post.

50
00:03:36,233 --> 00:03:38,703
Now the worst case with a tape.

51
00:03:39,153 --> 00:03:43,913
Each position is measured from the
last, and each reading is off by up

52
00:03:43,953 --> 00:03:48,143
to a millimeter either way,
drawn here twenty times larger.

53
00:03:48,773 --> 00:03:54,223
The errors add up along the post, and
every post adds them up differently.

54
00:03:54,767 --> 00:03:59,537
And here are the four story
stick posts, on the same scale.

55
00:03:59,987 --> 00:04:02,157
No difference between them at all.

56
00:04:07,233 --> 00:04:13,983
If the stick is a millimeter off, all
four posts are a millimeter off, together.

57
00:04:14,553 --> 00:04:17,123
The stick doesn't make the posts accurate.

58
00:04:17,573 --> 00:04:19,343
It makes them identical.

59
00:04:20,213 --> 00:04:23,063
And identical is what joinery needs:

60
00:04:23,513 --> 00:04:27,063
a joint only cares that
its two pieces agree.

61
00:04:30,233 --> 00:04:34,613
In the shop, a stick like
this is also called a bakabō:

62
00:04:35,033 --> 00:04:36,963
literally, a fool's stick.

63
00:04:37,473 --> 00:04:38,743
The usual explanation:

64
00:04:39,373 --> 00:04:43,083
with it, anyone gets the
same length, every time.

65
00:04:45,167 --> 00:04:48,837
A real building needs more than one stick:

66
00:04:49,687 --> 00:04:53,097
heights for the posts,
lengths along the walls.

67
00:04:54,067 --> 00:05:00,897
And the whole building is drawn out on a
board, the ita-zu, with every piece named.

68
00:05:01,687 --> 00:05:03,984
That board is episode three.

69
00:05:05,433 --> 00:05:06,643
Mark it once.

70
00:05:06,993 --> 00:05:08,523
Copy it everywhere.

71
00:05:09,193 --> 00:05:11,683
Pieces that agree with
each other will fit,

72
00:05:12,033 --> 00:05:14,163
even when they disagree with the tape.
